### What is the problem?

Given k sorted lists (arrays), merge them into one sorted list efficiently using a priority queue. The key insight is that each list is already sorted, so you only ever need to compare the current fronts of the k lists.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class MergeKSortedArrays {

    public static List<Integer> merge(int[][] lists) {
        // Entry: [value, listIndex, elementIndex]
        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e[0]));

        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
            if (lists[i].length > 0) {
                heap.offer(new int[]{lists[i][0], i, 0});
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            int[] top = heap.poll();
            result.add(top[0]);
            int li = top[1], ei = top[2];
            if (ei + 1 < lists[li].length) {
                heap.offer(new int[]{lists[li][ei + 1], li, ei + 1});
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] lists = {
            {10000, 30000, 50000},
            {20000, 40000},
            {15000, 25000, 60000}
        };
        System.out.println("Merged: " + merge(lists));
    }
}
```

```text
Merged: [10000, 15000, 20000, 25000, 30000, 40000, 50000, 60000]
```

### How does the source tracking work?

Each entry in the heap stores three values: the element value, the list index it came from, and the position within that list. When you poll an entry, you know which list to advance by reading its list index and incrementing the position.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class SourceTracking {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] lists = {{10, 30}, {20, 40}, {15, 25}};
        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e[0]));

        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
            heap.offer(new int[]{lists[i][0], i, 0});
        }

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            int[] top = heap.poll();
            System.out.println("Value=" + top[0]
                + " from list " + top[1] + " pos " + top[2]);
            if (top[2] + 1 < lists[top[1]].length) {
                heap.offer(new int[]{lists[top[1]][top[2]+1], top[1], top[2]+1});
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Value=10 from list 0 pos 0
Value=15 from list 2 pos 0
Value=20 from list 1 pos 0
Value=25 from list 2 pos 1
Value=30 from list 0 pos 1
Value=40 from list 1 pos 1
```

### How does this compare to the naive approach?

| Approach | Time | Space | Exploits sorted? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Concatenate + sort | O(N log N) | O(N) | No |
| Min-heap k-way | O(N log k) | O(k) | Yes |
| Pairwise merge | O(N log k) | O(1) | Yes |

### How is this used with BigDecimal for money?

For financial data, each list contains `BigDecimal` amounts. The heap's comparator uses `compareTo` instead of integer comparison.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class MergeBigDecimalLists {

    record Entry(BigDecimal value, int listIdx, int elemIdx) {}

    public static List<BigDecimal> merge(BigDecimal[][] lists) {
        PriorityQueue<Entry> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparing(Entry::value));

        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
            if (lists[i].length > 0) {
                heap.offer(new Entry(lists[i][0], i, 0));
            }
        }

        List<BigDecimal> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            Entry top = heap.poll();
            result.add(top.value());
            int next = top.elemIdx() + 1;
            if (next < lists[top.listIdx()].length) {
                heap.offer(new Entry(lists[top.listIdx()][next], top.listIdx(), next));
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[][] lists = {
            {new BigDecimal("10000.50"), new BigDecimal("30000.75")},
            {new BigDecimal("20000.25"), new BigDecimal("40000.00")}
        };
        System.out.println("Merged: " + merge(lists));
    }
}
```

```text
Merged: [10000.50, 20000.25, 30000.75, 40000.00]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time | O(N log k) |
| Space | O(k) for the heap |
| N | Total elements across all lists |
| k | Number of lists |

### How does this relate to external merge sort?

External merge sort splits a large file into sorted runs that fit in memory, then merges them using exactly this k-way merge. The heap holds one element per run, and the merge proceeds by polling the smallest and advancing that run. Banks use this to merge enormous sorted transaction files.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class ExternalMerge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("External merge sort:");
        System.out.println("1. Split large file into sorted chunks (runs)");
        System.out.println("2. Open k runs simultaneously");
        System.out.println("3. Use min-heap with one element per run");
        System.out.println("4. Poll smallest, write to output, read next from that run");
        System.out.println("5. Result: one fully sorted file");
    }
}
```

```text
External merge sort:
1. Split large file into sorted chunks (runs)
2. Open k runs simultaneously
3. Use min-heap with one element per run
4. Poll smallest, write to output, read next from that run
5. Result: one fully sorted file
```

### What is the pairwise merge alternative?

Instead of a heap, pair up the k lists and merge them two at a time in log k rounds. This also achieves O(N log k) but avoids the heap overhead and uses O(1) extra space for the merge itself.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class PairwiseAlternative {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pairwise merge:");
        System.out.println("Round 1: merge list 0+1, 2+3, 4+5 -> k/2 lists");
        System.out.println("Round 2: merge pairs again -> k/4 lists");
        System.out.println("...after log k rounds: 1 sorted list");
        System.out.println("Same O(N log k) time, no heap needed.");
    }
}
```

```text
Pairwise merge:
Round 1: merge list 0+1, 2+3, 4+5 -> k/2 lists
Round 2: merge pairs again -> k/4 lists
...after log k rounds: 1 sorted list
Same O(N log k) time, no heap needed.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistakes are: forgetting to check for empty lists when seeding the heap, not tracking which list an element came from (causing wrong advancement), and using a max-heap instead of a min-heap.
